  "text": "2019B12 Draw the oxygen-haemoglobin and carbon\ndioxide-haemoglobin dissociation curves\non the same axes (content vs. partial pressure). Compare and contrast these two\ncurves.\nList:\n·\nGraph\n·\nTable of normal values\n·\nTable of comparison\nNormal values:\nOxygen\nCarbon dioxide\nPa (mmHg)\n100\n40\nCa (mL/100mL)\n20\n48\nPv (mmHg)\n40\n46\nCv (mL/100mL)\n15\n52\na-v difference (mL/100mL)\n5\n4\nHCO\n3\n-\n60%\nCarbamino 30%\nDissolved 10%\nArterial storage\nHb-bound 98.5%\nDissolved 1.5% (\n∝\npartial\n  pressure)\nHCO\n3\n-\n90%\nCarbamino 5%\nDissolved 5% (\n∝\npartial\n  pressure)\nCurve comparison:\nOxygen\nCarbon dioxide\nShape\nSigmoid (law of mass action + co-operative binding)\nLinear\nSlope\nShallower\n-Large a-v difference in pO\n2\n-Large ↓PaO\n2\nin shunt\nSteeper\n-Small a-v difference in pCO\n2\n-Small ↑PaCO\n2\nin shunt\nShift\nRight shift -> ↓affinity if:\n-↑H\n+\n, ↑pCO\n2\n(Bohr effect)\n-↑[2,3-DPG]\n-↑Temp\nLeft shift -> ↑affinity if:\n-↓SO\n2\n(Haldane effect)\nUpper section\n-Implication for shunt\nFlat\n-↑V\nA\nand ↑PiO\n2\ncause minimal\n  improvement in oxygenation\nLinear\n-↑V\nA\neasily restores ventilation\nFeedback welcome at\nketaminenightmares@gmail.com",
